{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-180750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{n}^{4}:n\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={n}^{4},\\,n\\in Z={0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,…}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down some terms of our sequence until the pattern becomes clear.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{-3} & =(-3)^{4}=81 \\\\ {a}_{-2} & =(-2)^{4}=16 \\\\ {a}_{-1} & =(-1)^{4}=1 \\\\ {a}_{0} & ={0}^{4}=0 \\\\ {a}_{1} & ={1}^{4}=1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={2}^{4}=16 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={3}^{4}=81 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set consists of the elements\n\n$$\nA={0,1,16,81…}.\n$$\n\nThese are just the fourth powers including $0.$ Continuing the pattern, we have\n\n$$\nA={0,1,16,81,256,625,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "95d55ca3254675c8ee695c1eb22f5cc3a1cf8fe6cddaaafe9f0a79fb3c7874f8"} {"question" "q-180750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{0,1,16,32,54,81,…}"] ["math" "{0,1,16,81,256,625,…}"] ["math" "{0,1,16,81,256,625}"] ["math" "{0,1,16,81,256}"] ["math" "{0,1,9,16,25,36,…}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{0,1,16,81,256,625,…}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n}:n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n},\\,n\\in N={1,2,3,4,…}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{1}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(1)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{3}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{4}=\\frac{1}{2}(1)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur sequence alternates between $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $+\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "95d55ca3254675c8ee695c1eb22f5cc3a1cf8fe6cddaaafe9f0a79fb3c7874f8"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2},1}"] ["math" "{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},1}"] ["math" "{-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}}"] ["math" "{-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}}"] ["math" "{\\frac{1}{2},1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{n}^{3}:n\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={n}^{3},\\,n\\in Z={0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,…}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down some terms of our sequence until the pattern becomes clear.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{-3} & =(-3)^{3}=-27 \\\\ {a}_{-2} & =(-2)^{3}=-8 \\\\ {a}_{-1} & =(-1)^{3}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{0} & ={0}^{3}=0 \\\\ {a}_{1} & ={1}^{3}=1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={2}^{3}=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={3}^{3}=27 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set consists of the elements\n\n$$\nA={0,\\pm 1,\\pm 8,\\pm 27,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "95d55ca3254675c8ee695c1eb22f5cc3a1cf8fe6cddaaafe9f0a79fb3c7874f8"} {"question" "q-180740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-180740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{0,1,8,27,...}"] ["math" "{0,3,9,27}"] ["math" "{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,…}"] ["math" "