}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+3{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=1,\\,{b}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={b}_{2}+3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =4+3(1) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={b}_{3}+3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =7+3(4) \\\\  & =19 \\\\ {b}_{5} & ={b}_{4}+3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =19+3(7) \\\\  & =40 \\\\ {b}_{6} & ={b}_{5}+3{b}_{4} \\\\  & =40+3(19) \\\\  & =97 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={1,4,7,19,40,97,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-183672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "95d55ca3254675c8ee695c1eb22f5cc3a1cf8fe6cddaaafe9f0a79fb3c7874f8"} {"question" "q-183672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-183672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{1,4,7,14,21,28,…}"] ["math" "{1,4,7,14,28,35,…}"] ["math" "{1,4,7,19,28,63,…}"] ["math" "{1,4,7,19,40,64,…}"] ["math" "{1,4,7,19,40,97,…}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-183672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{1,4,7,19,40,97,…}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB={{b}_{n}\\,:\\,{b}_{n+1}=3{b}_{n}\\,,\\,{b}_{1}=2,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $B$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${b}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n+1}=3{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=2,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {b}_{3} & =3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(6) \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {b}_{4} & =3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =3(18) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={2,6,18,54,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "95d55ca3254675c8ee695c1eb22f5cc3a1cf8fe6cddaaafe9f0a79fb3c7874f8"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{2,6,18,22,…}"] ["math" "{2,6,18,24,…}"] ["math" "{2,6,18,32,…}"] ["math" "{2,6,18,54,…}"] ["math" "{2,6,18,58,…}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "q-181705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{2,6,18,54,…}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/state.json" "file_sha256" "a1015adf27d4e3e2ae3adee3bcf5ac092cc6922199e5c2f33bd99ae384d04bfc"} {"question" "e-13177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA = \\{ (-1)^n \\, n : n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\}.\n$$\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13177.json" "file_sha256" "bdd0db4a9e864464d574cfbbb22a2d8d4ad2460d49c1c1be0ab7cc3338bbe96e"} {"question" "e-13177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_n = (-1)^n \\,n, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N = \\{1,2,3,4,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =(-1)^{1}(1)=-1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =(-1)^{2}(2)=2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =(-1)^{3}(3)=-3 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =(-1)^{4}(4)=4 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ -1, 2, -3, 4,  \\ldots \\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13177.json" "file_sha256" "bdd0db4a9e864464d574cfbbb22a2d8d4ad2460d49c1c1be0ab7cc3338bbe96e"} {"question" "e-13142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA = \\left \\{ 4^n : n \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2  \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nDescribe $A$ by listing all of its elements." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13142.json" "file_sha256" "f77b93f7c76c822d258f9b5f9dedb1877179ca13ada4682d4d9d54a44fbaf917"} {"question" "e-13142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The condition after the \"$:$\" symbol, \n\n$$\nn \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2\n$$\n\nstates that $n$ can be any integer that satisfies the inequality $-2\\leq n\\leq 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_n = 4^n, \\qquad n \\in \\{0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's write down all the terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{-2} & ={4}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{-1} & ={4}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {a}_{0} & ={4}^{0}=1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & ={4}^{1}=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={4}^{2}=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{16},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,1,\\,4,\\,16 \\right\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13142.json" "file_sha256" "f77b93f7c76c822d258f9b5f9dedb1877179ca13ada4682d4d9d54a44fbaf917"} {"question" "e-13285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA = \\big \\{ a_n \\,:\\,a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - 1, \\, a_1 = 2,\\, n \\in \\mathbb N \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13285.json" "file_sha256" "ad320575ab707b7b5e81000d9c8a5ffb73e8792d12112e5c78ba50e4caf9093f"} {"question" "e-13285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =2a_{n} - 1, \\quad a_1 =2,\\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-1 \\\\  & =2(2)-1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2{a}_{2}-1 \\\\  & =2(3)-1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2{a}_{3}-1 \\\\  & =2(5)-1 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2{a}_{4}-1 \\\\  & =2(9)-1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{2,3,5,9,17,\\ldots \\right\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038029/example-13285.json" "file_sha256" "ad320575ab707b7b5e81000d9c8a5ffb73e8792d12112e5c78ba50e4caf9093f"}] "usage" {"e-13142" [] "q-183519" [] "e-13285" [] "q-180740" [] "e-13177" []} "basis" 1299 "retractions" [[17592186054978 :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = {n^{3}: n ∈ Z}$.\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A$?"] [17592186055002 :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n$A = {n^{2} + 2n: n ∈ N, n \\ge 2}$.\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A$?"] [17592186054354 :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n$A = \\{(-1)^n \\, n: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\}$.\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation."] [17592186054354 :question/worked-solution "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_n = (-1)^n \\,n, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N = \\{1,2,3,4,\\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= (-1)^{1}(1) =-1 \\\\\na_{2} &= (-1)^{2}(2) = 2 \\\\\na_{3} &= (-1)^{3}(3) =-3 \\\\\na_{4} &= (-1)^{4}(4) = 4 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{-1, 2, -3, 4, \\ldots \\}\n$$"] [17592186054345 :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = \\left \\{4^n: n \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2 \\right \\}$.\n\nDescribe $A$ by listing all of its elements."] [17592186054345 :question/worked-solution "The condition after the \"$$:\" symbol,\n\n$$\nn \\in \\mathbb Z,\\, -2\\leq n\\leq 2\n$$\n\nstates that $n$ can be any integer that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-2\\leq n\\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_n = 4^n, \\qquad n \\in \\{0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2\\}\n$$\n\nLet's write down all the terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{-2} &= 4^{-2} = \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\na_{-1} &= 4^{-1} = \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\na_{0} &= 4^{0} = 1 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4^{1} = 4 \\\\\na_{2} &= 4^{2} = 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{16},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,1,\\,4,\\,16 \\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186054363 :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = \\big \\{a_n \\,:\\,a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - 1, \\, a_1 = 2,\\, n \\in \\mathbb N \\big\\}$.\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation."] [17592186054363 :question/worked-solution "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =2a_{n} - 1, \\quad a_1 =2,\\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(2) - 1 \\\\\n&= 3 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2a_{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(3) - 1 \\\\\n&= 5 \\\\\na_{4} &= 2a_{3} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(5) - 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\\\\na_{5} &= 2a_{4} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(9) - 1 \\\\\n&= 17 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{2,3,5,9,17,\\ldots \\right\\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186051796 17592186051797 17592186051798 17592186051799 17592186051800 17592186051897 17592186051898 17592186051899 